      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\), we need to determine \\(3^{12000} \\mod 1000\\). We are given that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\). This means that the exponent 12000 can be expressed as \\(12000 = 3 \\cdot 400 + 200\\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[3^{12000} = (3^{400})^3 \\cdot 3^{200}\\]\n\nSince \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), we have:\n\n\\[(3^{400})^3 \\equiv 1^3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\nSo, we only need to find \\(3^{200} \\mod 1000\\). To do this, we can use Euler's theorem, which states that for any integer \\(a\\) and \\(n\\) that are coprime, \\(a^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{n}\\), where \\(\\phi\\) is the Euler's totient function. For \\(n = 1000\\), \\(\\phi(1000) = 1000 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 400\\). Therefore, \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), and since \\(200 < 400\\), we can directly compute \\(3^{200} \\mod 1000\\).\n\nWe can use successive squaring to find \\(3^{200} \\mod 1000\\):\n\n\\[3^2 = 9\\]\n\\[3^4 = 81\\]\n\\[3^8 = 6561 \\equiv 561 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\\[3^{16} = 561^2 = 314721 \\equiv 721 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\\[3^{32} = 721^2 = 519841 \\equiv 841 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\\[3^{64} = 841^2 = 707281 \\equiv 281 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\\[3^{128} = 281^2 = 78961 \\equiv 961 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\\[3^{200} = 3^{128} \\cdot 3^{72} = 961 \\cdot 721 = 696341 \\equiv 341 \\pmod{1000}\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(3^{12000} \\equiv 1 \\cdot 341 \\equiv 341 \\pmod{1000}\\). The last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\) are \\(\\boxed{341}\\).",
